rceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.31.4","value":"1Sam.31.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"31:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," David had once held the position of ",{"c":["armor bearer"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.16.21","r":"1Sam.16.21","rawR":"1Sam.16.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.16.21","value":"1Sam.16.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"16:21"},"). • ",{"c":["kill me:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This is one of many parallels between the death of Abimelech in the time of the judges (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.9.1-57","r":"Judg.9.1-57","rawR":"Judg.9.1-57","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.9.1-57","value":"Judg.9.1-57"}],"t":"ref","v":"Judg 9"},") and Saul, Israel’s first national king (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Judg.9.54","r":"Judg.9.54","rawR":"Judg.9.54","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.9.54","value":"Judg.9.54"}],"t":"ref","v":"Judg 9:54"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.31.4","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":31,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":31,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.31.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[31],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c749df39773085de726af93359d482f9b067fe4314577492d6640670f131658c","sourceItemName":"ISam.31.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3648,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.31.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.31.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.31.8","r":"1Sam.31.8","rawR":"1Sam.31.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.31.8","value":"1Sam.31.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"31:8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The victorious army would ",{"c":["strip the dead"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," to search for anything valuable (such as clothing, weapons, or jewelry) on the corpses."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.31.8","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":31,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":31,"startVerse":8}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.31.8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[31],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"103cffe221fc70b71a78eec376814f9f80b379c6b481af75786ff275da71009d","sourceItemName":"ISam.31.8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3649,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.31.8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.31.8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.31.9","r":"1Sam.31.9","rawR":"1Sam.31.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.31.9","value":"1Sam.31.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"31:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," In the ancient world, the death of an enemy king was ",{"c":["good news"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," for it meant that the enemy’s god had been defeated. However, little did the Philistines know that their victory would be short-lived and hollow. David, the Lord’s true anointed king, would soon come to power and pay back the Philistines mightily. Far from defeating Israel’s God, the Philistines were simply fulfilling his purpose."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.31.9","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":31,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":31,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.31.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[31],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5ab64d41c5d0e74f472ca495a3e3b3027d540b6163026d1c68d40ef8be3a521d","sourceItemName":"ISam.31.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3650,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.31.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.31.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.31.10","r":"1Sam.31.10","rawR":"1Sam.31.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.31.10","value":"1Sam.31.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"31:10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["the Ashtoreths:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," See ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.7.3","r":"1Sam.7.3","rawR":"1Sam.7.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.7.3","value":"1Sam.7.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:3"},". • ",{"c":["fastened his body:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This practice further degraded the executed person by depriving him of proper burial. It also served as a deterrent to other potential enemies (see also ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.40.19","r":"Gen.40.19","rawR":"Gen.40.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.40.19","value":"Gen.40.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 40:19"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.21-22","r":"Deut.21.21-22","rawR":"Deut.21.21-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.21-22","value":"Deut.21.21-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 21:21-22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.10.26","r":"Josh.10.26","rawR":"Josh.10.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.10.26","value":"Josh.10.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 10:26"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Esth.9.6-14","r":"Esth.9.6-14","rawR":"Esth.9.6-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Esth.9.6-14","value":"Esth.9.6-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Esth 9:6-14"},"). • ",{"c":["Beth-shan"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was a short distance east of Mount Gilboa."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.31.10","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":31,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":31,"startVerse":10}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.31.10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[31],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"93c44a4462cbaa6e2862c717611d5b9048b75966a52c1346fb5ad7df86349435","sourceItemName":"ISam.31.10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3651,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.31.10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.31.10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.31.11","r":"1Sam.31.11","rawR":"1Sam.31.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.31.11","value":"1Sam.31.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"31:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["people of Jabesh-gilead"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," were returning the favor Saul had done for them when he saved them from the Ammonites and their king Nahash (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.11.1-13","r":"1Sam.11.1-13","rawR":"1Sam.11.1-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.11.1-13","value":"1Sam.11.1-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"11:1-13"},"). Jabesh-gilead was about twelve miles southeast of Beth-shan and across the Jordan River."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.31.11","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":31,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":31,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.31.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[31],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"1a2c7608f603d7035b5c4b4d27556b25b26c3894992dafaed4c69ece0ac79664","sourceItemName":"ISam.31.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3652,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.31.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.31.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.31.12","r":"1Sam.31.12","rawR":"1Sam.31.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.31.12","value":"1Sam.31.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"31:12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["burned the bodies:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Cremation was rare. It is possible the bodies were burned because decomposition had already set in."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.31.12","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":31,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":31,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.31.12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[31],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e19c63a859c64c204898dafdbc4de0d85f04d712de1be0584885a0313ea4b87d","sourceItemName":"ISam.31.12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3653,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.31.12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.31.12","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.31.13","r":"1Sam.31.13","rawR":"1Sam.31.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.31.13","value":"1Sam.31.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"31:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["bones . . . Jabesh:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This was not the final resting place of Saul’s remains (see ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.21.12-14","r":"2Sam.21.12-14","rawR":"2Sam.21.12-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.21.12-14","value":"2Sam.21.12-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sam 21:12-14"},"). • The ending of 1 Samuel brings Israel’s history back to the situation at the end of Judges, when “Israel had no king.” Once again, the Philistines were in control. David had God’s anointing but no crown. He had wives but no sons. His affiliation with the hated Philistines might jeopardize his standing among his own people. David’s future path would be challenging, but God was clearly with him."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:ISam.31.13","refs":[{"book":"1SA","endChapter":31,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":31,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"ISam.31.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"1SA","sourceChapterCoverage":[31],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"889b11bf2d95e7038a79970044f4e06455f3c68a2c841da9ab543071d0034d8d","sourceItemName":"ISam.31.13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":3654,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"1SA","canonicalOSIS":"1Sam","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"1Sam"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"1Sam.31.13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"ISam.31.13","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}